                'type' => 'report',
                'category' => 'Digital inclusion',
                'excerpt' => 'A practical review of the choices that make online learning easier to join, follow, and complete across different devices and needs.',
                'body' => "Executive summary\n\nOnline learning becomes more usable when accessibility is planned as part of the course, rather than added after the lessons are finished. Clear navigation, readable materials, captions, flexible participation, and predictable course routines support a wider range of learners.\n\nAreas reviewed\n\nThis demonstration report examines five parts of the learning experience: finding a course, understanding lesson materials, taking part in live sessions, completing activities, and getting help. The review uses a practical checklist based on common inclusive-design principles. It does not represent a new survey or an audit of a specific learning platform.\n\nFindings\n\nThe most useful improvements are often small and consistent. Give pages descriptive headings, ensure controls work with a keyboard, provide captions or transcripts for recorded media, and share session materials in advance. Explain deadlines and participation expectations in plain language. Offer an alternative way to contribute when speaking live is difficult.\n\nA course should also make support visible. Learners need to know where to ask a question, how quickly to expect a reply, and what to do if a tool or format does not work for them.\n\nRecommended actions\n\n1. Test the full learner journey on a phone and with a keyboard.\n2. Add captions and accessible notes to every recorded class.\n3. Use a predictable structure for lessons, tasks, and feedback.\n4. Ask learners what barriers they encounter and publish what changed.\n\nLimitations\n\nThis sample report is an editorial guide, not original empirical research. The recommendations should be checked against applicable accessibility standards and the needs of the specific learner community.",
