                'excerpt' => 'A structured way to check what a chart measures, where its data came from, and which comparisons the evidence can support.',
                'body' => "Purpose\n\nA dashboard can make complex information easier to explore, but a chart is only as useful as its definitions and context. This sample report offers a repeatable reading method for public dashboards across health, education, environment, and local services.\n\nRead in four passes\n\nFirst, identify the question the dashboard is trying to answer. Next, check the unit, population, time period, and geography behind each measure. Then inspect the source and update date. Finally, look for missing groups, changes in methodology, and uncertainty that could affect a comparison.\n\nWhat to record\n\nFor each chart, note the indicator definition, denominator, collection method, time coverage, and any exclusions. Avoid comparing totals when populations differ, or treating a change in reporting as a change in real-world outcomes. When a dashboard does not explain a measure, treat that as an evidence gap.\n\nRecommended practice\n\nKeep a link to the source data, preserve the chart's notes, and describe conclusions in proportion to the evidence. Use more than one source when a decision has meaningful consequences. If the data is provisional or incomplete, state that plainly.\n\nScope and limitations\n\nThis guide does not analyze a particular dataset or claim a finding about any sector. It is a demonstration report intended to help readers ask better questions of public information.",
