                'excerpt' => 'Why stronger learning evidence combines practice, feedback, reflection, and work samples instead of relying on completion alone.',
                'body' => "Overview\n\nA completed course shows participation, but it does not always show what a learner can do. A stronger picture of capability comes from observing applied work, the decisions behind it, and how a learner responds to feedback.\n\nA practical evidence model\n\nUse several kinds of evidence together: a short knowledge check for core concepts, a realistic task for application, a portfolio item that makes the work visible, and a reflection that explains the learner's choices. Where possible, give learners a clear rubric before they begin.\n\nFeedback and revision\n\nFeedback is most useful when it is specific, timely, and connected to a next step. Allowing a revision helps distinguish a first attempt from the capability developed through practice. Mentors should explain both what is working and what would make the work more effective.\n\nFair interpretation\n\nA single project can be affected by access to time, tools, prior experience, or support. Avoid treating one score as a complete measure of a person's potential. Use consistent criteria, provide reasonable alternatives, and let learners describe relevant context.\n\nLimitations\n\nThis demonstration report presents a framework, not a validated assessment instrument or labor-market study. Organizations should pilot assessment methods with learners and employers before using them for high-stakes decisions.",
