                'excerpt' => 'A concise planning framework for clear consent, useful questions, responsible data handling, and sharing findings back with a community.',
                'body' => "Why research design matters\n\nCommunity research asks people to spend time and share perspectives. A thoughtful process makes the purpose clear, asks only what is useful, and explains what will happen after responses are collected.\n\nBefore inviting participants\n\nWrite down the decision the research will inform. Define who needs to participate and why. Keep questions neutral and specific, test the form with a small group, and state the expected time commitment. Explain whether participation is anonymous, how long information will be kept, and who will review it.\n\nDuring collection\n\nOffer accessible formats and avoid collecting personal details that are not needed. Make required questions clearly visible. Give participants a way to skip optional questions and a contact route for concerns. Protect response data with access controls and a retention plan.\n\nAfter collection\n\nSummarize patterns without overstating what a small or self-selected group can tell you. Share the findings in plain language and explain what actions followed. When the results are uncertain, say so.\n\nLimitations\n\nThis sample report is general guidance and is not legal or ethics-board advice. Research involving sensitive topics, vulnerable groups, or regulated data may require specialist review.",
